Focuses the warning before http's button on Windows only while the warning
shows. Chrome focuses it as the dialog is made, even for a tab that isn't
showing, whose dialog the tab dialog manager shows inactive and hides at once.
The core gives a tab's dialog a window of its own on Windows alone
(PageDialogsInOwnWindow), and focusing a view in a hidden window activates it
there, so an agent's tab at the warning took the keyboard from the user's
window (#3164). The inactive show already stores the button for the dialog's
activation, which focuses it as the tab comes forward. On Linux and the Mac
Chrome's focus stays: on Linux a click on the warning once it shows gives the
keyboard to the button focused here, and without it the click left the
keyboard in the UI.

--- a/chrome/browser/ssl/ask_before_http_dialog_controller.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ssl/ask_before_http_dialog_controller.cc
@@ -254,7 +254,21 @@
   // Explicitly call RequestFocus() to ensure initially focused view is focused.
   views::View* focused_view = model_host->GetInitiallyFocusedView();
   CHECK(focused_view);
-  focused_view->RequestFocus();
+  // browser_core: on Windows, only while the dialog shows. A dialog of a tab
+  // that isn't showing was shown inactive, which stored this view for its
+  // activation, and hidden. The core gives a tab's dialog a window of its own
+  // on Windows alone, and focusing a view in it activates that hidden window,
+  // so it took the keyboard from the window the user was in (#3164). Elsewhere
+  // Chrome's focus stays: on Linux a click on the dialog once it shows gives
+  // the keyboard to the view focused here, and without it the click left the
+  // keyboard in the UI.
+  bool focus = true;
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
+  focus = dialog_widget_->IsVisible();
+#endif
+  if (focus) {
+    focused_view->RequestFocus();
+  }
 #else
   current_dialog_model_ = dialog_model.get();
   ui::ModalDialogWrapper::ShowTabModal(std::move(dialog_model), window);
